    "created_at": "2017-07-25T19:30:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-07-25T19:30:00Z",
    "body": "Yes, this shouldn't be referring to rendering concepts.  It looks like it's *trying*, informally, to define that canvases default to `1px` per backing store pixel (that is, the `canvas`’s *intrinsic size* is equal to the width/height of the backing store, in CSS px), and that the dimensions increase going right/down.\r\n\r\nThat is, the CSS concept is a result of canvas concepts, not the other way around as this text seems to be stating.\r\n\r\n> Isn't it incorrect when you can have `<canvas width=200 height=200 style=width:100px;height:100px></canvas>`?\r\n\r\nYup. The canvas's intrinsic size is 200×200, but the rendered size has nothing to do with this.\r\n\r\n> And if backed by a 3x display, would be drawn on 300x300x device pixels (creating some artifacts due to the scaling).\r\n\r\nYup.",
